[[:db/retract 17592186222005 :question/answer-fields 17592186220704] [:db/retract 17592186222005 :question/answer-fields 17592186220709] {:db/id 17592186221417 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-45a54fce-a708-5a31-9c28-340df3e868af" "question-1391978c-3f23-5276-a4d7-85ffc5372cc1"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221182 :question/problem "Given the system of differential equations, determine whether the system is almost linear at the equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast= [0, \\: 0]^T.$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-{y}^{2}(t)+1-{e}^{x(t)} \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-{x}^{2}(t)+1-{e}^{y(t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}' = \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x})\n$$\n\nwhere ![](images/d8/d80eb6b2beaebedf071ab77f752156bfd4b433f3762deefc008751fbf0fd24f4.svg) and \n![](images/a8/a8e250dcd16fe545e136b5b58697f8ebf9ee674c44d31bebd5d4e9b67eb61a96.svg)\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n![](images/5f/5f841f13bd8e85cbac3b84dc57a3e9edaeef97ddedfa271e6befba2c97131015.svg)\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{y}^{2}+1-{e}^{x})=-{e}^{x},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{y}^{2}+1-{e}^{x})=-2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{x}^{2}+1-{e}^{y})=-2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{x}^{2}+1-{e}^{y})=-{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n![](images/77/77748e3e4d751bc1e9f6f2f8979440e46dd820e0263b893993ee8fd1bcdae50c.svg)\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ![](images/9b/9b03c0103e4d9b884776d63daa94a28cf078ec8e84bdaaf183744b6d4a493d75.svg) to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J(0,0) & =|\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{0} & -2(0) \\\\ -2(0) & -{e}^{0}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)(-1)-(0)(0) \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian determinant is not zero, our system is $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{almost linear}}$ at $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221418 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e8e22bce-e115-5d92-9ec4-991287c11960" "question-4189cf97-4019-5e9f-9f66-1a0dc740de8d" "question-056d7f87-a110-577b-a370-409d88e311c9" "question-ba7562c9-dc1e-5d77-b34b-c5d59c44924f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221181 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)+3y(t)-4x(t)+4 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)-2x(t)+{y}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the corresponding linearized system at the equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast=[2, \\ 0]^T?$" :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}' = \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x})\n$$\n\nwhere ![](images/d4/d422a2e5dc28d7f3163879aa16f212b3573e9f2eb20f6aa1dd28ce2e8b55466b.svg) and \n![](images/2d/2df6f3b3a0c55d34c93291a33fca3f3f33f61a7491beb4cc495540a0f5ab5ae6.svg)\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n![](images/a5/a59de210f69e55b9de475da491dc1de9f32ccad7253ab1e4ab70714b96210e6d.svg)\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+3y-4x+4)=2x-4,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+3y-4x+4)=3 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-2x+{y}^{2})=2x-2,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-2x+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n![](images/28/2832518b9ff9a0795b8b1f337a92fbd97b8b1628da52ee88a2a9c88d95c330bc.svg)\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ![](images/0e/0e9e15cf4c6eb8e07e42b3c32fffe458c230511e50e02650f8c9bf4226801b5a.svg) to get\n\n![](images/cf/cf167eaf02d0500310e31db9b3db546c3dcd1b395438bdf5daaeefec2af500ae.svg)\n\nFinally, near the equilibrium, the nonlinear system is approximated by\n\n![](images/39/39b57ee5a860cfd3b73c64bebf93a77a193339530ae6970f931972a2e345a1df.svg)\n\nwhere the deviation vector is\n\n![](images/3c/3c80deae8395322a7abb6e106ccf084428b93e00e9b8087f53aa0c8bb76c00e4.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221419 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4c33868d-8235-5c25-b35a-535f5738b142" "question-e9194ecf-6556-5bbf-bbe7-0cf26c275fb8" "question-74a51538-7992-5c00-9322-0a9614a20c67" "question-73b75bfd-1216-5131-9b02-68e4ee57dee1" "question-3df14087-9cbc-58b7-a996-90852883ca3a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221183 :question/problem "![](images/86/8635a08be7b8fa85e73b8683e0f17b95f9c834116d9ac29267d2ee5e98002856.svg)\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the behavior of the system’s solutions near the equilibrium ![](images/dc/dc2fe4cdba6d5eab5c763479aa564b60cf7c5c4736d958e7341223c30a580aab.svg)" :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n![](images/f8/f876ff09a7b8c0bfd16d6597389967749eeade8305ada19fe4770d575705c601.svg)\nwhere ![](images/f6/f60e24b209fce8d306850ca088ebbc091fb9ddd11cda74e46ac7e2dc9cc61610.svg) and \n![](images/57/572bf982c2dfc04ad3b0771d666e9956b5e316e9148574c53788fc66ca9cfd35.svg)\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of ![](images/41/41df3ddfc93bfc6b75f58565e44fde8667b575eab70d34a6ad26f0fc7d7b9f00.svg)\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n![](images/ea/eacac468e4ce8b3ea07d93ddfb7f6336d8a3f6a822bedaffa07cd5431aa31a1d.svg)\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n![](images/8a/8aac1cd3e1f495a9f509442a8599882e61092618f03e43431d68b7e807d65c69.svg)\n\nThus, we have:\n\n![](images/83/83cdeef9e17081a8236ac8d14ea9d8b8fda3da046d95f84fadbbf93d72a9bc0a.svg)\n\nNow, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ![](images/81/81720438d92024448a479c3488e0478f5de9a4b26c59544fa738ca2cf6c1e6d7.svg) to get\n\n![](images/1b/1b42df3f7cf42a954e20ade84aeb884235bf693c1c03258ed316f4b13cbc01e6.svg)\n\nNotice that the matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), so the system is almost linear at ![](images/00/009ee734f37f8813ce80ea9e89d4156cb498825273cf0f21e52818218ea99434.svg)\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n![](images/d4/d4a328abcc87b1a35120504bb31e047476aa18af01cef1498964d2a4b777f201.svg)\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues\n![](images/74/74dd38ba634c0a0d0150d29c4bc21320be4372679610877f8dab85b4dedc9182.svg)\n\nFinally, since we have two real eigenvalues with opposite signs, our equilibrium is an ![](images/92/921a21d96fcb849ac36e1369ed9798e1c895e2549aa9e3643da1198d8403d556.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-45a54fce-a708-5a31-9c28-340df3e868af" :question/id #uuid "45a54fce-a708-5a31-9c28-340df3e868af" :question/math-academy-id "q-336882" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)+y(t)-5 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={y}^{2}(t)+x(t)-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the system of differential equations, select the correct options below.\n\nThe Jacobian determinant of the system at the equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}=[2,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ is given as\n\n$$\nJ(2,1)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nAt this point, the system is \n{{field-2}}\nat ${x}^{∗}.$" :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+y-5 \\\\ {y}^{2}+x-3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\nJ=|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+y-5)=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+y-5)=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+x-3)=1,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+x-3)=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nJ=|\\begin{aligned}2x & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2y\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J(2,1) & =|\\begin{aligned}2(2) & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2(1)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =7 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian determinant is not zero, our system is $\\text{almost linear}$ at ${x}^{∗}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5fa690cb-f556-55fa-af0b-ad088f3e0b28" "answer-field-2a3ff5ab-5195-5904-838a-34e77d3c3d2c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5fa690cb-f556-55fa-af0b-ad088f3e0b28" :answer-field/id #uuid "5fa690cb-f556-55fa-af0b-ad088f3e0b28" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-934d5518-0836-5d37-8cc1-567ae23d217f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ea6ca74f-2f76-5dbf-a29a-a13e77e73e61" "answer-f5dbd0c7-4811-55a5-be98-82a11c521cdb" "answer-4e44e2d9-fd5b-5274-a4c1-fe92c4c28ddb" "answer-934d5518-0836-5d37-8cc1-567ae23d217f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ea6ca74f-2f76-5dbf-a29a-a13e77e73e61" :answer/id #uuid "ea6ca74f-2f76-5dbf-a29a-a13e77e73e61" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f5dbd0c7-4811-55a5-be98-82a11c521cdb" :answer/id #uuid "f5dbd0c7-4811-55a5-be98-82a11c521cdb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4e44e2d9-fd5b-5274-a4c1-fe92c4c28ddb" :answer/id #uuid "4e44e2d9-fd5b-5274-a4c1-fe92c4c28ddb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}0 & \\pi  \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-934d5518-0836-5d37-8cc1-567ae23d217f" :answer/id #uuid "934d5518-0836-5d37-8cc1-567ae23d217f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2a3ff5ab-5195-5904-838a-34e77d3c3d2c" :answer-field/id #uuid "2a3ff5ab-5195-5904-838a-34e77d3c3d2c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-8d2afa48-09df-57de-91de-9c0cb5eb84df" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8d2afa48-09df-57de-91de-9c0cb5eb84df" "answer-9235f6c0-3319-580d-b3bf-fdbbd06f932b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8d2afa48-09df-57de-91de-9c0cb5eb84df" :answer/id #uuid "8d2afa48-09df-57de-91de-9c0cb5eb84df" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "almost linear" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9235f6c0-3319-580d-b3bf-fdbbd06f932b" :answer/id #uuid "9235f6c0-3319-580d-b3bf-fdbbd06f932b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "not almost linear" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186368108 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y(t)) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={y}^{2}(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x(t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the system of differential equations, select the correct options below.\n\nThe Jacobian determinant of the system at the equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}=[1,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ is given as\n\n$$\nJ(1,1)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nAt this point, the system is \n{{field-2}}\nat ${x}^{∗}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186222005 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)+{y}^{2}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)-{y}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the system of differential equations, select the correct options below.\n\nThe Jacobian determinant of the system at the equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\text{ }0]^{T}$ is given as\n\n$$\nJ(0,0)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nAt this point, the system is \n{{field-2}}\nat ${x}^{∗}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186368125 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y(t)) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={y}^{2}(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x(t))-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the system of differential equations, select the correct options below.\n\nThe Jacobian determinant of the system at the equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\text{ }0]^{T}$ is given as\n\n$$\nJ(0,0)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nAt this point, the system is \n{{field-2}}\nat ${x}^{∗}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-1391978c-3f23-5276-a4d7-85ffc5372cc1" :question/id #uuid "1391978c-3f23-5276-a4d7-85ffc5372cc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-334933" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-{y}^{2}(t)+1-{e}^{x(t)} \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-{x}^{2}(t)+1-{e}^{y(t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the system of differential equations, select the correct options below.\n\nThe Jacobian determinant of the system at the equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\text{ }0]^{T}$ is given as\n\n$$\nJ(0,0)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nAt this point, the system is \n{{field-2}}\nat ${x}^{∗}.$" :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{2}+1-{e}^{x} \\\\ -{x}^{2}+1-{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\nJ=|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{y}^{2}+1-{e}^{x})=-{e}^{x},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{y}^{2}+1-{e}^{x})=-2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{x}^{2}+1-{e}^{y})=-2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{x}^{2}+1-{e}^{y})=-{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nJ=|\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{x} & -2y \\\\ -2x & -{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J(0,0) & =|\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{0} & -2(0) \\\\ -2(0) & -{e}^{0}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian determinant is not zero, our system is $\\text{almost linear}$ at ${x}^{∗}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5e0bda42-6407-5b29-9afc-20a9efb70a23" "answer-field-8d48c5f0-1607-5c9c-9667-b921f37fbbb8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5e0bda42-6407-5b29-9afc-20a9efb70a23" :answer-field/id #uuid "5e0bda42-6407-5b29-9afc-20a9efb70a23" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-900d00fb-6d70-57d8-b664-39cb2b3658bd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-900d00fb-6d70-57d8-b664-39cb2b3658bd" "answer-518db6f0-044f-53a1-b4ac-02b876a8ce5d" "answer-8408a1d3-b7f2-5609-8b87-660697d4c0b6" "answer-75de21b8-e810-56ba-aa15-d9778b4ddb7e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-900d00fb-6d70-57d8-b664-39cb2b3658bd" :answer/id #uuid "900d00fb-6d70-57d8-b664-39cb2b3658bd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-518db6f0-044f-53a1-b4ac-02b876a8ce5d" :answer/id #uuid "518db6f0-044f-53a1-b4ac-02b876a8ce5d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}-\\pi  & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8408a1d3-b7f2-5609-8b87-660697d4c0b6" :answer/id #uuid "8408a1d3-b7f2-5609-8b87-660697d4c0b6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-75de21b8-e810-56ba-aa15-d9778b4ddb7e" :answer/id #uuid "75de21b8-e810-56ba-aa15-d9778b4ddb7e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}0 & \\pi  \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8d48c5f0-1607-5c9c-9667-b921f37fbbb8" :answer-field/id #uuid "8d48c5f0-1607-5c9c-9667-b921f37fbbb8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-82c7071e-b6e9-5a86-bb8e-b48b3bc6df5e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-82c7071e-b6e9-5a86-bb8e-b48b3bc6df5e" "answer-551b9ac3-2040-5138-8370-255e0e43ce5b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-82c7071e-b6e9-5a86-bb8e-b48b3bc6df5e" :answer/id #uuid "82c7071e-b6e9-5a86-bb8e-b48b3bc6df5e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "almost linear" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-551b9ac3-2040-5138-8370-255e0e43ce5b" :answer/id #uuid "551b9ac3-2040-5138-8370-255e0e43ce5b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "not almost linear" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e8e22bce-e115-5d92-9ec4-991287c11960" :question/id #uuid "e8e22bce-e115-5d92-9ec4-991287c11960" :question/math-academy-id "q-336902" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+3x(t)+2y(t)-1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)+y(t)-4x(t)+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the corresponding linearized system at the equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}=[1,\\text{ }-1]^{T}?$\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${u}^{′}=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"50px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"50px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,u,\\,$ where $u=x-{x}^{∗}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+3x+2y-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+y-4x+4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+3x+2y-1)=2x+3,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+3x+2y-1)=-2y+2 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+y-4x+4)=2x-4,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+y-4x+4)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}2x+3 & -2y+2 \\\\ 2x-4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}2(1)+3 & -2(-1)+2 \\\\ 2(1)-4 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, near the equilibrium, the nonlinear system is approximated by\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]u,\n$$\n\nwhere the deviation vector is\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y+1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-025cfb1a-50d5-5082-8686-5fcbb1c3f912" "answer-field-180c8d44-02cf-5931-b8b4-81414f9c43e3" "answer-field-3ccc26cc-c67c-5c0e-a63d-47074ce83aa5" "answer-field-0c34426b-2fc9-5fbf-b2d2-279d5189a4db"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-025cfb1a-50d5-5082-8686-5fcbb1c3f912" :answer-field/id #uuid "025cfb1a-50d5-5082-8686-5fcbb1c3f912" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-5c9c348d-a36b-53dd-81bb-ef33120ab054" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5c9c348d-a36b-53dd-81bb-ef33120ab054"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5c9c348d-a36b-53dd-81bb-ef33120ab054" :answer/id #uuid "5c9c348d-a36b-53dd-81bb-ef33120ab054" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-180c8d44-02cf-5931-b8b4-81414f9c43e3" :answer-field/id #uuid "180c8d44-02cf-5931-b8b4-81414f9c43e3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-daef1d20-d35f-5e42-a590-ffdbe947eed0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-daef1d20-d35f-5e42-a590-ffdbe947eed0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-daef1d20-d35f-5e42-a590-ffdbe947eed0" :answer/id #uuid "daef1d20-d35f-5e42-a590-ffdbe947eed0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3ccc26cc-c67c-5c0e-a63d-47074ce83aa5" :answer-field/id #uuid "3ccc26cc-c67c-5c0e-a63d-47074ce83aa5" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-a68e2c01-d079-5ccc-a595-ded49893f847" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a68e2c01-d079-5ccc-a595-ded49893f847"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a68e2c01-d079-5ccc-a595-ded49893f847" :answer/id #uuid "a68e2c01-d079-5ccc-a595-ded49893f847" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0c34426b-2fc9-5fbf-b2d2-279d5189a4db" :answer-field/id #uuid "0c34426b-2fc9-5fbf-b2d2-279d5189a4db" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-f7c2e02c-9726-5a93-bbda-a22a6bf7595c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f7c2e02c-9726-5a93-bbda-a22a6bf7595c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f7c2e02c-9726-5a93-bbda-a22a6bf7595c" :answer/id #uuid "f7c2e02c-9726-5a93-bbda-a22a6bf7595c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4189cf97-4019-5e9f-9f66-1a0dc740de8d" :question/id #uuid "4189cf97-4019-5e9f-9f66-1a0dc740de8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-366655" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)-2x(t)+3y(t)+3 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4x(t)+2{y}^{2}(t)-y(t)+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the corresponding linearized system at the equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}=[2,\\text{ }-1]^{T}?$\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${u}^{′}=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"50px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"50px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,u,\\,$ where $u=x-{x}^{∗}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x+3y+3 \\\\ -4x+2{y}^{2}-y+5\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-2x+3y+3)=2x-2,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-2x+3y+3)=3 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-4x+2{y}^{2}-y+5)=-4,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-4x+2{y}^{2}-y+5)=4y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}2x-2 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 4y-1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}2(2)-2 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 4(-1)-1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -4 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, near the equilibrium, the nonlinear system is approximated by\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -4 & -5\\end{aligned}]u,\n$$\n\nwhere the deviation vector is\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x-2 \\\\ y+1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ed8eb70b-344a-5050-b370-92b4d15051d8" "answer-field-4a0a7ee5-4574-567f-ae65-cd2e7676f0b8" "answer-field-1cbfa53a-6d93-5264-b814-7e6efcdd6d4b" "answer-field-194dfd1f-21e6-59b2-9593-2beb7f9e8250"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ed8eb70b-344a-5050-b370-92b4d15051d8" :answer-field/id #uuid "ed8eb70b-344a-5050-b370-92b4d15051d8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-080e961a-297c-5b7d-9c41-af81d7198804" :answer-field/choices ["answer-080e961a-297c-5b7d-9c41-af81d7198804"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-080e961a-297c-5b7d-9c41-af81d7198804" :answer/id #uuid "080e961a-297c-5b7d-9c41-af81d7198804" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4a0a7ee5-4574-567f-ae65-cd2e7676f0b8" :answer-field/id #uuid "4a0a7ee5-4574-567f-ae65-cd2e7676f0b8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-ee096123-d43d-5356-8566-8623f265bf9b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ee096123-d43d-5356-8566-8623f265bf9b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ee096123-d43d-5356-8566-8623f265bf9b" :answer/id #uuid "ee096123-d43d-5356-8566-8623f265bf9b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1cbfa53a-6d93-5264-b814-7e6efcdd6d4b" :answer-field/id #uuid "1cbfa53a-6d93-5264-b814-7e6efcdd6d4b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-4aa59ac8-cd8f-54fc-832d-b165c63d5079" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4aa59ac8-cd8f-54fc-832d-b165c63d5079"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4aa59ac8-cd8f-54fc-832d-b165c63d5079" :answer/id #uuid "4aa59ac8-cd8f-54fc-832d-b165c63d5079" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-194dfd1f-21e6-59b2-9593-2beb7f9e8250" :answer-field/id #uuid "194dfd1f-21e6-59b2-9593-2beb7f9e8250" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-86448ffa-4985-5006-92fe-7235fceb5be8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-86448ffa-4985-5006-92fe-7235fceb5be8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-86448ffa-4985-5006-92fe-7235fceb5be8" :answer/id #uuid "86448ffa-4985-5006-92fe-7235fceb5be8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-056d7f87-a110-577b-a370-409d88e311c9" :question/id #uuid "056d7f87-a110-577b-a370-409d88e311c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-335136" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+2{y}^{2}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4x(t)+{x}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the corresponding linearized system at the equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\text{ }0]^{T}?$\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${u}^{′}=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"50px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"50px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,u,\\,$ where $u=x-{x}^{∗}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}-x+2{y}^{2} \\\\ -4x+{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-x+2{y}^{2})=-1,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-x+2{y}^{2})=4y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-4x+{x}^{2})=-4+2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-4x+{x}^{2})=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4y \\\\ -4+2x & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4(0) \\\\ -4+2(0) & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, near the equilibrium, the nonlinear system is approximated by\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}]u,\n$$\n\nwhere the deviation vector is\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d9c48156-6c36-5682-8a0c-533f385ce593" "answer-field-eac3dd97-a737-5044-a0f0-cdc9088f977e" "answer-field-e6b282bb-52e2-5ba2-92ff-89f8fe1cc11d" "answer-field-ebfec07f-2b8f-5c9f-a779-f1652bdc2150"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d9c48156-6c36-5682-8a0c-533f385ce593" :answer-field/id #uuid "d9c48156-6c36-5682-8a0c-533f385ce593" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-a74585fc-43b7-580d-95e5-5d075a0f7994" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a74585fc-43b7-580d-95e5-5d075a0f7994"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a74585fc-43b7-580d-95e5-5d075a0f7994" :answer/id #uuid "a74585fc-43b7-580d-95e5-5d075a0f7994" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-eac3dd97-a737-5044-a0f0-cdc9088f977e" :answer-field/id #uuid "eac3dd97-a737-5044-a0f0-cdc9088f977e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-047a0fe4-098e-5b1b-85f7-8eea9cbfcd48" :answer-field/choices ["answer-047a0fe4-098e-5b1b-85f7-8eea9cbfcd48"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-047a0fe4-098e-5b1b-85f7-8eea9cbfcd48" :answer/id #uuid "047a0fe4-098e-5b1b-85f7-8eea9cbfcd48" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e6b282bb-52e2-5ba2-92ff-89f8fe1cc11d" :answer-field/id #uuid "e6b282bb-52e2-5ba2-92ff-89f8fe1cc11d" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-e991a0bf-9f6b-5211-a7d0-c91413fe8845" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e991a0bf-9f6b-5211-a7d0-c91413fe8845"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e991a0bf-9f6b-5211-a7d0-c91413fe8845" :answer/id #uuid "e991a0bf-9f6b-5211-a7d0-c91413fe8845" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ebfec07f-2b8f-5c9f-a779-f1652bdc2150" :answer-field/id #uuid "ebfec07f-2b8f-5c9f-a779-f1652bdc2150" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-814de77e-70f6-5947-9487-b60887214561" :answer-field/choices ["answer-814de77e-70f6-5947-9487-b60887214561"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-814de77e-70f6-5947-9487-b60887214561" :answer/id #uuid "814de77e-70f6-5947-9487-b60887214561" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ba7562c9-dc1e-5d77-b34b-c5d59c44924f" :question/id #uuid "ba7562c9-dc1e-5d77-b34b-c5d59c44924f" :question/math-academy-id "q-335146" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={y}^{2}(t)-7x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)+6y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the corresponding linearized system at the equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\text{ }0]^{T}?$\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${u}^{′}=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"50px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"50px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,u,\\,$ where $u=x-{x}^{∗}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-7x \\\\ {x}^{2}+6y\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-7x)=-7,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-7x)=2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+6y)=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+6y)=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2y \\\\ 2x & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2(0) \\\\ 2(0) & 6\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, near the equilibrium, the nonlinear system is approximated by\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]u,\n$$\n\nwhere the deviation vector is\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-437ce4a5-5dc9-5925-ae4d-585c8c8f74fc" "answer-field-93a1d6d3-7477-5a08-8996-177851628c79" "answer-field-09f65387-8ba8-5141-ba8a-df8f2bbbf3ab" "answer-field-9e03c506-fce9-59d6-a650-e23e814065e8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-437ce4a5-5dc9-5925-ae4d-585c8c8f74fc" :answer-field/id #uuid "437ce4a5-5dc9-5925-ae4d-585c8c8f74fc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-e30ed290-68c5-52dc-965d-511f089e8620" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e30ed290-68c5-52dc-965d-511f089e8620"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e30ed290-68c5-52dc-965d-511f089e8620" :answer/id #uuid "e30ed290-68c5-52dc-965d-511f089e8620" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-93a1d6d3-7477-5a08-8996-177851628c79" :answer-field/id #uuid "93a1d6d3-7477-5a08-8996-177851628c79" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-5de99c57-aa35-5b2f-a607-d5829c37aa45" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5de99c57-aa35-5b2f-a607-d5829c37aa45"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5de99c57-aa35-5b2f-a607-d5829c37aa45" :answer/id #uuid "5de99c57-aa35-5b2f-a607-d5829c37aa45" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-09f65387-8ba8-5141-ba8a-df8f2bbbf3ab" :answer-field/id #uuid "09f65387-8ba8-5141-ba8a-df8f2bbbf3ab" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-f32677a8-f34a-553b-9a82-9ec020d11dc6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f32677a8-f34a-553b-9a82-9ec020d11dc6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f32677a8-f34a-553b-9a82-9ec020d11dc6" :answer/id #uuid "f32677a8-f34a-553b-9a82-9ec020d11dc6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9e03c506-fce9-59d6-a650-e23e814065e8" :answer-field/id #uuid "9e03c506-fce9-59d6-a650-e23e814065e8" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-b4ff3df2-4cd8-543e-85f5-87acb8ccb94f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b4ff3df2-4cd8-543e-85f5-87acb8ccb94f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b4ff3df2-4cd8-543e-85f5-87acb8ccb94f" :answer/id #uuid "b4ff3df2-4cd8-543e-85f5-87acb8ccb94f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186368184 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)+x(t)y(t)-2x(t)+3y(t)+5 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={y}^{2}(t)-x(t)+4y(t)+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the corresponding linearized system at the equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}=[1,\\text{ }-1]^{T}?$\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${u}^{′}=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"50px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"50px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,u,\\,$ where $u=x-{x}^{∗}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-4c33868d-8235-5c25-b35a-535f5738b142" :question/id #uuid "4c33868d-8235-5c25-b35a-535f5738b142" :question/math-academy-id "q-336918" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=y(t)+(x(t)-2)^{3}+8 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)+4+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y(t)+8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the behavior of the system's solutions near the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[2,\\text{ }-8]^{T}?$\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium is an \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}y+(x-2)^{3}+8 \\\\ -2x+4+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+8)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y+(x-2)^{3}+8)=3(x-2)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2x(t)+4+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y(t)+8))=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2x+4+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+8))=-2 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2x(t)+4+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y(t)+8))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}3(x-2)^{2} & 1 \\\\ -2 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+8)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[2,\\text{ }-8]^{T}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}3((2)-2)^{2} & 1 \\\\ -2 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(-8+8)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}0-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-2 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda +2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}i.$\n\nSince we have two complex eigenvalues with positive real parts, our equilibrium is an $\\text{unstable}$ $\\text{spiral source}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ec7e64f0-46b4-5113-827e-d2299e0897e2" "answer-field-43333bf7-6c4e-5722-bb18-63f5f2d05e3b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ec7e64f0-46b4-5113-827e-d2299e0897e2" :answer-field/id #uuid "ec7e64f0-46b4-5113-827e-d2299e0897e2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-6014dc91-5d52-5025-b8e6-08bc57c4a993" :answer-field/choices ["answer-51dc4f1a-b8ef-5510-bb03-91610013ce6a" "answer-6014dc91-5d52-5025-b8e6-08bc57c4a993"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-51dc4f1a-b8ef-5510-bb03-91610013ce6a" :answer/id #uuid "51dc4f1a-b8ef-5510-bb03-91610013ce6a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "asymptotically stable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6014dc91-5d52-5025-b8e6-08bc57c4a993" :answer/id #uuid "6014dc91-5d52-5025-b8e6-08bc57c4a993" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "unstable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-43333bf7-6c4e-5722-bb18-63f5f2d05e3b" :answer-field/id #uuid "43333bf7-6c4e-5722-bb18-63f5f2d05e3b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-4194eb5b-e8e8-52bf-ae3f-640f35c26071" :answer-field/choices ["answer-35c3e72e-755c-5855-9dc1-693bcd430713" "answer-ac89dec7-3711-5332-981c-e5af7f6e5723" "answer-4194eb5b-e8e8-52bf-ae3f-640f35c26071"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-35c3e72e-755c-5855-9dc1-693bcd430713" :answer/id #uuid "35c3e72e-755c-5855-9dc1-693bcd430713" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "saddle" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ac89dec7-3711-5332-981c-e5af7f6e5723" :answer/id #uuid "ac89dec7-3711-5332-981c-e5af7f6e5723" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "spiral sink" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4194eb5b-e8e8-52bf-ae3f-640f35c26071" :answer/id #uuid "4194eb5b-e8e8-52bf-ae3f-640f35c26071" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "spiral source" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e9194ecf-6556-5bbf-bbe7-0cf26c275fb8" :question/id #uuid "e9194ecf-6556-5bbf-bbe7-0cf26c275fb8" :question/math-academy-id "q-336934" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t)+y(t)^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2y(t)+x(t)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the behavior of the system's solutions near the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\text{ }0]^{T}?$\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium is an \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}-3x+{y}^{2} \\\\ -2y+{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-3x+{y}^{2})=-3 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-3x+{y}^{2})=2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2y+{x}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y+{x}^{2})=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2y \\\\ 2x & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get \n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2(0) \\\\ 2(0) & -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), so the system is almost linear at ${x}^{∗}.$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-3-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-3-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=-2,\\text{ }-3.$\n\nFinally, since we have two real and negative eigenvalues, our equilibrium is an $\\text{asymptotically stable}$ $\\text{sink}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b3c47420-898c-5eed-a1f6-36f38aa9d41e" "answer-field-bc7db006-7c89-5d8f-a5ab-4403236f4ab0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b3c47420-898c-5eed-a1f6-36f38aa9d41e" :answer-field/id #uuid "b3c47420-898c-5eed-a1f6-36f38aa9d41e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-b9a66a2a-e65d-5a2b-ad02-2948fa1cd356" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b9a66a2a-e65d-5a2b-ad02-2948fa1cd356" "answer-b71bb514-1902-5366-9c65-2802bd8b7d2b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b9a66a2a-e65d-5a2b-ad02-2948fa1cd356" :answer/id #uuid "b9a66a2a-e65d-5a2b-ad02-2948fa1cd356" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "asymptotically stable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b71bb514-1902-5366-9c65-2802bd8b7d2b" :answer/id #uuid "b71bb514-1902-5366-9c65-2802bd8b7d2b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "unstable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bc7db006-7c89-5d8f-a5ab-4403236f4ab0" :answer-field/id #uuid "bc7db006-7c89-5d8f-a5ab-4403236f4ab0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-4e392284-bf67-5773-ac84-6acd62f23a6a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4e392284-bf67-5773-ac84-6acd62f23a6a" "answer-ddecb96b-c156-543c-beb1-5817228d02ac" "answer-fc27b79b-7e6b-5b85-a343-6623191d10ed"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4e392284-bf67-5773-ac84-6acd62f23a6a" :answer/id #uuid "4e392284-bf67-5773-ac84-6acd62f23a6a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "sink" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ddecb96b-c156-543c-beb1-5817228d02ac" :answer/id #uuid "ddecb96b-c156-543c-beb1-5817228d02ac" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "source" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fc27b79b-7e6b-5b85-a343-6623191d10ed" :answer/id #uuid "fc27b79b-7e6b-5b85-a343-6623191d10ed" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "spiral sink" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-74a51538-7992-5c00-9322-0a9614a20c67" :question/id #uuid "74a51538-7992-5c00-9322-0a9614a20c67" :question/math-academy-id "q-336926" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}+y-1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3)-y-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the behavior of the system's solutions near the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}={[0,\\text{ }1]}^{T}?$\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium is an \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+y-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3)-y-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-1)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+y-1)=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+y-1)=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3)-y-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-1))=\\frac{1}{x+3} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3)-y-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-1))=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}2x & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{x+3} & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), so the system is almost linear at ${x}^{∗}.$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-\\lambda )(-1-\\lambda )-\\frac{1}{3} & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda +1)-\\frac{1}{3} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -\\frac{1}{3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{6}.$\n\nFinally, since we have two real eigenvalues with opposite signs, our equilibrium is an $\\text{unstable}$ $\\text{saddle}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f625c762-bad3-507e-8e40-bec7a29d8f5f" "answer-field-8fed120f-3cd0-5b43-b0e2-666006741843"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f625c762-bad3-507e-8e40-bec7a29d8f5f" :answer-field/id #uuid "f625c762-bad3-507e-8e40-bec7a29d8f5f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-c2ba3315-c686-565d-8e5a-63bc28f08104" :answer-field/choices ["answer-602cf795-4031-5bfc-83b2-fe8ca785f3b4" "answer-c2ba3315-c686-565d-8e5a-63bc28f08104"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-602cf795-4031-5bfc-83b2-fe8ca785f3b4" :answer/id #uuid "602cf795-4031-5bfc-83b2-fe8ca785f3b4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "asymptotically stable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c2ba3315-c686-565d-8e5a-63bc28f08104" :answer/id #uuid "c2ba3315-c686-565d-8e5a-63bc28f08104" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "unstable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8fed120f-3cd0-5b43-b0e2-666006741843" :answer-field/id #uuid "8fed120f-3cd0-5b43-b0e2-666006741843" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-60730a3e-e949-57de-8ec2-bc261b9d848e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-60730a3e-e949-57de-8ec2-bc261b9d848e" "answer-62e277f3-b18b-56f4-aa90-8bad13a1e90b" "answer-f69fa633-4c70-541f-a6c4-8d60a3b1d403"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-60730a3e-e949-57de-8ec2-bc261b9d848e" :answer/id #uuid "60730a3e-e949-57de-8ec2-bc261b9d848e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "saddle" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-62e277f3-b18b-56f4-aa90-8bad13a1e90b" :answer/id #uuid "62e277f3-b18b-56f4-aa90-8bad13a1e90b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "spiral sink" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f69fa633-4c70-541f-a6c4-8d60a3b1d403" :answer/id #uuid "f69fa633-4c70-541f-a6c4-8d60a3b1d403" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "spiral source" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-73b75bfd-1216-5131-9b02-68e4ee57dee1" :question/id #uuid "73b75bfd-1216-5131-9b02-68e4ee57dee1" :question/math-academy-id "q-336683" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x(t))+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y(t)^{2}+1) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5y(t)+{tan}^{-1}⁡(x(t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the behavior of the system's solutions near the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\text{ }0]^{T}?$\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium is an \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1) \\\\ 5y+{tan}^{-1}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1))=2+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1))=\\frac{2y}{{y}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5y+{tan}^{-1}⁡(x))=\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5y+{tan}^{-1}⁡(x))=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}2+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & \\frac{2y}{{y}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}} & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}2+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) & \\frac{2(0)}{(0)^{2}+1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{1+(0)^{2}} & 5\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), so the system is almost linear at ${x}^{∗}.$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=3,\\text{ }5.$\n\nFinally, since we both have two real and positive eigenvalues,, our equilibrium is an $\\text{unstable}$ $\\text{source}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-eab9dd67-f41e-5506-a8b5-c3347f7c7560" "answer-field-5319ccbb-ca29-5b39-970f-99bd75a0ebcc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-eab9dd67-f41e-5506-a8b5-c3347f7c7560" :answer-field/id #uuid "eab9dd67-f41e-5506-a8b5-c3347f7c7560" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-f87b2376-9c6e-540a-b6f2-620442d5299a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-682cd089-8e13-55c2-9498-40b7761fee9a" "answer-f87b2376-9c6e-540a-b6f2-620442d5299a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-682cd089-8e13-55c2-9498-40b7761fee9a" :answer/id #uuid "682cd089-8e13-55c2-9498-40b7761fee9a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "asymptotically stable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f87b2376-9c6e-540a-b6f2-620442d5299a" :answer/id #uuid "f87b2376-9c6e-540a-b6f2-620442d5299a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "unstable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5319ccbb-ca29-5b39-970f-99bd75a0ebcc" :answer-field/id #uuid "5319ccbb-ca29-5b39-970f-99bd75a0ebcc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-f0fe5180-e59f-52de-85cf-407940214623" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f0fe5180-e59f-52de-85cf-407940214623" "answer-96be3107-0cb4-5d1a-b521-44976f754743" "answer-c556e800-1066-566b-bf43-0cfcdca7d9cc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f0fe5180-e59f-52de-85cf-407940214623" :answer/id #uuid "f0fe5180-e59f-52de-85cf-407940214623" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "source" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-96be3107-0cb4-5d1a-b521-44976f754743" :answer/id #uuid "96be3107-0cb4-5d1a-b521-44976f754743" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "spiral sink" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c556e800-1066-566b-bf43-0cfcdca7d9cc" :answer/id #uuid "c556e800-1066-566b-bf43-0cfcdca7d9cc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "spiral source" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3df14087-9cbc-58b7-a996-90852883ca3a" :question/id #uuid "3df14087-9cbc-58b7-a996-90852883ca3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-336937" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-4x(t)-2x(t)^{3}+y(t)^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3y(t)-3y(t)^{3}+x(t)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the behavior of the system's solutions near the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\text{ }0]^{T}?$\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium is an \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and \n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=[\\begin{aligned}-4x-2{x}^{3}+{y}^{2} \\\\ -3y-3{y}^{3}+{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-4x-2{x}^{3}+{y}^{2})=-4-6{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-4x-2{x}^{3}+{y}^{2})=2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-3y-3{y}^{3}+{x}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-3y-3{y}^{3}+{x}^{2})=-3-9{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}-4-6{x}^{2} & 2y \\\\ 2x & -3-9{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get \n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}-4-6(0)^{2} & 2(0) \\\\ 2(0) & -3-9(0)^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), so the system is almost linear at ${x}^{∗}.$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-4-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-4-\\lambda )(-3-\\lambda ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=-3,\\text{ }-4.$\n\nFinally, since we have two real and negative eigenvalues, our equilibrium is an $\\text{asymptotically stable}$ $\\text{sink}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1f060b0a-5cae-57bf-bdcf-51f7b86e81a7" "answer-field-d8486c7e-8a0c-5692-a6e8-5d35730ed341"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1f060b0a-5cae-57bf-bdcf-51f7b86e81a7" :answer-field/id #uuid "1f060b0a-5cae-57bf-bdcf-51f7b86e81a7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-991487ac-27be-5ba6-b735-2aebd986386d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-991487ac-27be-5ba6-b735-2aebd986386d" "answer-ee31c528-c262-58b1-b5d9-40faa88c9ba1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-991487ac-27be-5ba6-b735-2aebd986386d" :answer/id #uuid "991487ac-27be-5ba6-b735-2aebd986386d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "asymptotically stable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ee31c528-c262-58b1-b5d9-40faa88c9ba1" :answer/id #uuid "ee31c528-c262-58b1-b5d9-40faa88c9ba1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "unstable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d8486c7e-8a0c-5692-a6e8-5d35730ed341" :answer-field/id #uuid "d8486c7e-8a0c-5692-a6e8-5d35730ed341" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-2d6ad51e-f8fe-58e6-9d7d-79e8fd9d36c3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2d6ad51e-f8fe-58e6-9d7d-79e8fd9d36c3" "answer-610c3938-ff33-5c6b-9f7e-26e4e51f1564" "answer-56dc24d0-4973-5f7a-bb8c-a5b05670f2a0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2d6ad51e-f8fe-58e6-9d7d-79e8fd9d36c3" :answer/id #uuid "2d6ad51e-f8fe-58e6-9d7d-79e8fd9d36c3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "sink" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-610c3938-ff33-5c6b-9f7e-26e4e51f1564" :answer/id #uuid "610c3938-ff33-5c6b-9f7e-26e4e51f1564" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "source" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-56dc24d0-4973-5f7a-bb8c-a5b05670f2a0" :answer/id #uuid "56dc24d0-4973-5f7a-bb8c-a5b05670f2a0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "spiral sink" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186222494 :tutorial/content "Consider an autonomous system written as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'=\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}),\n$$\n\nwhere ![](images/60/60be83e58a72728b03ebfa2de6999ec80f17ee29e3f2ab3060d8ca43bbf45de8.svg) and ![](images/98/98bb67f212fa8deee63da1b69ae93086b9eb56a3ec64d70ba36e6a94dff1527b.svg).\n\nThe system is called **almost linear** at an equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ if the Jacobian determinant at this point is nonzero there, i.e.\n\n$$\n\\det\\!\\bigl(\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast)\\bigr)\\neq 0.\n$$\n\nFor example, let\n![](images/e6/e640b7ca9ddc8252223ec70f011147a702cd25bca0ee8a20314693695c1bec40.svg)\n\nFirst, notice that $\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast)=\\mathbf{0},$ so $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is an equilibrium.\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y+{y}^{2})=1,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y+{y}^{2})=1+2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y)=1,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n![](images/93/93058061ca42daa7114b615bcb77a8f9ccbc9cdccec98559db3eb288a7208940.svg)\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ![](images/72/72feeca197610c24c70b01f4c1075c75eebe89ae29f1aced83caeb252c275cce.svg) to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J(0,0) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1+2(0) \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian is $0,$ the system is *not* almost linear at $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186222496 :tutorial/content "Suppose the system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'=\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}),\n$$\n\nis almost linear at an equilibrium $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast.$ In this case, the system's behavior near $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ can be approximated by a linear system.\n\nFirst, we define the deviation from equilibrium by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{x}^\\ast.\n$$\n\nSmall values of $\\mathbf{u}$ correspond to points close to the equilibrium.\n\nNext, assuming $\\mathbf{x} = [x, y]^T,$ we compute the Jacobian matrix\n![](images/7a/7a0ff677dae0e51e34aed63faadfcb31f277f060c03488fdc1828cf8a2a5a0ec.svg)\nand evaluate it at $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ to obtain the constant matrix $J = \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast).$\n\nFinally, since $\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast)=\\mathbf{0},$ the nonlinear system can be approximated by the **linearized system**\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}'= J \\mathbf{u}.\n$$\n\nThis linear system captures the leading-order behavior of solutions close to the equilibrium.\n\nLet's see an example." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186222495 :tutorial/content "Again, suppose $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is an equilibrium of the nonlinear system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'=\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}),\n$$\n\nand assume the system is *almost linear* at $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$. If \n\n$$\nJ=\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast)\n$$\n\ndenotes the Jacobian matrix evaluated at the equilibrium, then the behavior of solutions near $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is determined by the eigenvalues of $J.$ \n\nThis information can be summarized in the following table.\n\n| **Eigenvalues of the Jacobian** | **Behavior** | **Stability** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| real and both positive | source / unstable node | unstable |\n| real and both negative | sink / stable node | asymptotically stable |\n| real and opposite signs | saddle | unstable |\n| complex with positive real part | spiral source | unstable |\n| complex with negative real part | spiral sink | asymptotically stable |\n\nLet's now consider a concrete example." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186222497 :tutorial/content "In the classification above, we intentionally skipped one case: \n\nThe eigenvalues $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2$ of the Jacobian at the equilibrium are purely imaginary. \n\nIn this situation, the corresponding linearized system has a *center* at the origin, meaning that solutions neither grow nor decay, but instead circulate around the equilibrium.\n\nFor the original nonlinear system, however, this case is *inconclusive*. While the linearized system predicts closed orbits, the nonlinear system *may* exhibit a stable center, an unstable spiral, or other nearby behavior.\n\nTherefore, when the Jacobian has purely imaginary eigenvalues, linearization alone is not sufficient to classify the equilibrium. In such cases, further analysis beyond linear approximation is required." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221470 :activity/expected-seconds 1140.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
